            <ArticleTitle>Pain control after major surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>269-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the adequacy and efficacy of postoperative pain management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A prospective clinical study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Georgetown and New Amsterdam Public Hospitals, Guyana.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two hundred consecutive patients undergoing major abdominal surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Presence or absence of significant postoperative pain during the first 24 hours.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All the patients experienced pain postoperatively. Sixty one per cent of patients considered their pain severe, 30% rated it moderate and only 9% mild. Reasons for this deficiency of care are partly attributable to the patients themselves and also the health care staff.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Postoperative pain is poorly managed in our general hospitals.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
